  "marks": 6,
  "topic": "geo-t5",
  "topicConfident": true,
  "inSpec": true,
  "text": "Explain why hard engineering may increase the risk of river flooding in some\nplaces.\n Turn over",
  "ms": "Indicative content\nnumber Explain why hard engineering may increase the risk of river flooding\nin some places.\nAO1 (6 marks)\nAO1\n• Channelisation speeds up flow of water downstream and so reduces the\nheight of the flood wave but can transfer flood water from one\nprotected area to another unprotected area causing flooding for one\ncommunity at the expense of another.\n• Levees encourage development in floodplains and so increase risk.\n• If levees fail the subsequent flood wave is far higher and more\ndestructive than if the levees had not been constructed.\n• The construction of diversion spill ways can increase flood risk at the\npoint where the spillway re-joins the river as a result of increased\ndischarge.\n• As with channelisation areas further downstream are at greater risk of\nflooding as all of the flood wave is transferred downstream.\n• The building of wing dikes such as on the river Mississippi which slows\ndown channel flow has been linked to the increasing risk of river\nflooding.\n• Allow increased risk of flooding upstream due to the development of\nmega dams in times of heavy rain.\n• Allow increased risk of flooding caused by the development of hard\nengineering during the construction phase.\n• Reward those candidates who establish that this is in only some\nplaces and hard engineering can reduce the flood risk in other areas.\nAccept other explanations of why hard engineering may\nincrease the risk of river flooding in some places.\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1 0\nLevel 2 1–2 No rewardable material.\nLevel 3 3–4 • Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge\nand understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. (AO1)\n5–6\n• Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical\nideas. (AO1)\n• Understanding of geographical ideas lacks detail. (AO1)\n• Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding,\nwhich is mostly relevant and may include some\ninaccuracies. (AO1)\n• Understanding addresses a range of geographical\nideas. (AO1)\n• Understanding of geographical ideas is not fully detailed\nand/or developed. (AO1)\n• Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge\nand understanding throughout. (AO1)\n• Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical\nideas. (AO1)\n• Understanding of the geographical ideas is detailed and fully\ndeveloped. (AO1)",
  "pageFrom": 23,
  "pageTo": 23,
