  "topic": "geo-t4b",
  "topicConfident": true,
  "inSpec": false,
  "text": "(i) Suggest one reason why the population structures of Newcastle‑upon‑Tyne\nand Northumberland differ.\n(ii) Suggest reasons for the different levels of cultural diversity between\nNewcastle‑upon‑Tyne and Northumberland.",
  "ms": "Suggest one reason why the population structure of Northumberland and Mark\nnumber Newcastle-upon-Tyne differ.\nAO1 (2 marks)/AO2 (1 mark) (3)\nAward 1 mark for analysing the resource to identify one reason for the\ndifferences in population pyramids and a further 2 marks for expansion up\nto a maximum of 3 marks. For example:\n• Internal migration has led to key differences in the population\npyramids (1) with young people migrating to Newcastle for education\nopportunities at the University (1) and older residents migrating to the\nrural areas for retirement/quiet lifestyle (1).\n• Higher % older people in Northumberland than Newcastle (top of\npyramid much slimmer for Newcastle) as it is a rural area and people\nhave moved there (1) for a quiet/safe lifestyle or retirement (1) and\nyoung have moved away for employment/ education/ social\nopportunities. (1)\n• Higher % in age category 18-35 in Newcastle as students move there\nfor education/work from surrounding areas/elsewhere in country (1)\nand remain there for employment (1) and money circulates within\nlocal economy creating further attraction for other working age\npeople (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.\nSuggest reasons for the different levels of cultural diversity between Newcastle-upon-\nnumber Tyne and Northumberland.\nAO1 (3 marks)/AO2 (3 marks)\nSpecific knowledge about the places is not required.\nAO1\n• International and internal migration is changing the cultural characteristics of\nplaces.\n• There is variation in population characteristics (age, gender and ethnicity), both in\nAO2 and between settlements.\n• The vast majority of international migrants will end up in urban areas due to the\nvariety of job opportunities available, leading to greater cultural diversity.\n• More migrants both internal and international in Newcastle, bringing their own\nculture and expectations of life style, e.g. students.\n• New shops, places of worship, eating places to reflect this.\n• Wider range of cultural diversity in urban areas as population density enables this\nas more spending will take place, sustaining the shops.\n• Social clustering occurs as people feel safer within their language/culture/religious\ngroup.\n• Fewer opportunities for employment in rural areas, so clusters are fewer and\nsmaller and so attract fewer extra migrants.\n• Ageing population in rural areas resulting in less services aimed at the\neconomically active migrants.\nAccept any other appropriate response.\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1 0\nLevel 2 1–2 No rewardable material.\nLevel 3\n3–4 • Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and\nunderstanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. (AO1)\n5–6\n• Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which\nlack detail. (AO1)\n• Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is\nmostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. (AO1)\n• Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not\nfully detailed and/or developed. (AO1)\n• Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and\nunderstanding throughout. (AO1)\n• Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are\ndetailed and fully developed. (AO1)",
