  "text": "Assess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for\nsuperpower status.\n\n Turn over\nShaping Places",
  "ms": "Assess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for\nnumber superpower status.\nAnswer\nAO1 (3 marks)/AO2 (9 marks)\nResponses that demonstrate only AO1 without any AO2 should be awarded\nmarks as follows:\n• Level 1 AO1 performance: 1 mark\n• Level 2 AO1 performance: 2 marks\n• Level 3 AO1 performance: 3 marks.\nAO1\n• A superpower is a country with the ability to project its dominating power\nand influence anywhere in the world.\n• Emerging superpowers are countries with a large role in one of more\nsuperpower characteristics, and with growing influence. E.g. China,\nBrazil, India and Russia\n• There are six superpower characteristics; ecomomic i.e. GDP, Political i.e.\nvoting power in IGOs, miltiary i.e. blue water and nuclear, cultural i.e.\nmedia and TNCs, demographc i.e. population size and resources i.e.\nenergy\n• Challenges facing superpowers are tensions over acquistion of physical\nresources, issues surrounding counterfeiting, overlapping spheres of\ninfluence, cultural, political and environmental tensions\nAO2\nCountry Strengths Weaknesses\nBrazil • Abundant natural • Lacking economic\nresources e.g. iron ore, infrastructure i.e. poor\ntimber, soya, crude oil investment in road and rail\n• Produces half of South • Very high lending interest\nAmerica’s GDP so could rate around 16.25%\nbe classes as a regional • Spends 60% of South\nsuperpower America’s miltiary budget,\n• It is self sufficient in food however their military is\nand energy meaning it is the least signficant of all of\nenergy secure and less the BRICs.\neffected by ongoing global • They are reliant of primary\ninflation.\nproducts for export rather\n• Set to be the world’s fifth- than manufacturing rather\nlarges economy by 2050 than adding value to boost\novertaking Japan, their economy\nAssess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for\nnumber superpower status.\nAnswer Germany and Russia. • Politically less stable with\nRussia accusations of corruption\nwhich could deter FDI\nIndia\n• Wealth of natural • Russia’s population has\nChina resources e.g. oil, natural decreased between 1991-\ngas, coal 2015 and has seen high\nlosses due to the recent\n• Skilled workforce conflict.\n• World largest country by • The economy is unbalanced\nlandmass with the elite controlling a\nlarge prportion of Russia’s\nwealth.\n• Much of Russia’s aircraft\nand naval stock is ageing\nreducing their hard power\n• High levels of economic\nsanctions due to Ukrainian\ninvasion\n• Current political isolation by\nthe Western world\n• World’s largest democracy • Large public debt as a\nwith 672 million voters percentage of GDP (+74%)\n• Bollywood is the world’s which has led to the\nlargest film industry, reduxtion in the value of the\nproducing around 1200 rupee\nfilms a year • India has had high level of\n• Second largest population environmentla problems\nin the world meaning they with severe pollution\nhave a large working • The world’s largest emitter\npopulation to help boost of carbon dioxide\nthe economy. • 20% of Indians live in\n• Strong IT and service severe poverty, therefore it\nsector struggles to attract migrants\n• Founding member of the • Bollywood does not have the\nUN same global reach as\nHollywood reducing their\n• Some natural resources soft power.\ne.g. coal and natural gas\n• The world’s largest army • Environmental issues\nand the have clear becoming an obstacle to\nstrengths in submarines sustainable growth\nand combat aircafts • Increasing income\nresulting in strong hard\ninequality resulting in social\npower.\ntension\n• Communist one-party • The population is ageing\nstate with a very strong due to the previous one\nmanufacturing base child policy. By 2040, a\nallowing it to become the quarter of Chinese will be\n‘workshop of the world’. over 60.\nAssess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for\nnumber superpower status.\nAnswer\n• The largest population in • The open door policy to FDI\nthe world therefore is has has resulted in a heavy\nthe benefits of being a reliance on foreign markets\n‘demographic’ superpower making it vulnerable to\n• Industrially competitive external global factors.\n• Strong foreign direct • The Covid pandemic\nhighlighted cracks in\ninvestment\nChina’s healthcare system\n• Large deposits of coal, iron\nand combined with an\nore, petroleum and natural\ngas ageing population this could\ncause social problems in\nthe future.\nAssessment:\n• Best responses will consider how likely the chosen superpowers will be in\nchallenging for superpower status and whether they are likely to\ndominant the current superpower of the US.\n• Candidates may consider that Brazil can be considered a regional\nsuperpowers due to the fact that they dominate their respective\ncontinents in terms of economic power, but currently don’t dominate\nglobally.\n• Candidates may consider the challenges facing emerging superpowers in\nchallenging for superpower status and consider which challenge is more\nsignificant.\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1 0\n1–4 No rewardable material.\nLevel 2\n5–8 • Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and\nunderstanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\n(AO1)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas, making limited logical\nconnections/relationships. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas to produce an interpretation with limited\nrelevance and/or support. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas to make unsupported or generic judgements\nabout the significance of few factors, leading to an argument is\nunbalanced or lacks coherence. (AO2)\n• Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding,\nwhich is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies.\n(AO1)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas logically, making some relevant\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 3 9–12\nconnections/relationships. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas to produce a partial but coherent\ninterpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by\nevidence. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas to make judgements about the significance of\nsome factors, to produce an argument that may be unbalanced\nor partially coherent. (AO2)\n• Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge\nand understanding throughout. (AO1)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas logically, making relevant\nconnections/relationships. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas to produce a full and coherent interpretation\nthat is relevant and supported by evidence. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical\ninformation/ideas to make supported judgements about the\nsignificance of factors throughout the response, leading to a\nbalanced and coherent argument. (AO2)",
