  "topic": "geo-syn",
  "topicConfident": true,
  "inSpec": true,
  "text": "Study Figure 3 in Section B of the Resource Booklet, which shows data for\nthree countries.\nAnalyse the differences in level of development for the three countries shown.\n*S49796A0512* Pearson Edexcel Level 3 Advanced GCE in Geography – Sample Assessment Materials – 5\n137\nIssue 2 – February 2017 © Pearson Education Limited 2017",
  "ms": "Indicative content\nnumber\nAO1 (4 marks)/AO3 (4 marks)\nAO1 development can be assessed using a range of measures, both human\n• and economic but there can be issues of reliability with both single\nmeasures and indices to measure development\n• indices such as Gini Coefficient which is the most commonly used\nmeasure of a nations inequality may be used as they show more detail\n• then single measures\n• economic development such as a higher income per person, can lead to\nAO3 further developments such as access to electricity\n• youthful populations are likely to have high birth rates and low economic\ndevelopment\n•\n• Angola’s economy is growing much faster than the DRC/Congo; answers\nmight argue this does not indicate level of development, only progress\n• from a human development perspective, Angola looks weaker than\nCongo; it has a very high infant mortality rate (suggesting poor\nhealthcare/access) and a low life expectancy – Congo is a full six years’\nmore\ncredit the idea that, from Figure 5, Angola is almost wholly reliant on oil\nexports, which might suggest a very ‘simple’ economy\nanswers might refer to the Gini Co-efficient, which is very high for\nAngola, suggesting a very unequal society – perhaps meaning the per\ncapita income data is of little value\na case can be made for Congo being the most developed; its per capita\nincome is in the middle and the % living on less than $1.25 per day is\nthe lowest of the three countries; income distribution is better than\nAngola and life expectancy, access to electricity and infant mortality are\nthe best; its 43% population 0–14 might suggest it is transitioning\ntowards a higher development level\nAccept any other appropriate response.\nPearson Edexcel Level 3 Advanced GCE in Geography – Sample Assessment Materials – 11599\nIssue 2 – February 2017 © Pearson Education Limited 2017\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1\n0 No rewardable material.\n1–3 • Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge\nand understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or\nLevel 2 4–6 irrelevant. (AO1)\n• Investigates the question/issue to produce a limited analysis\nLevel 3 7–8 of data/evidence, making few connections to geographical\nideas. (AO3)\n• Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding,\nwhich is mostly relevant but may include some inaccuracies.\n(AO1)\n• Critically investigates the question/issue to produce an\nanalysis of data/evidence, making some logical connections\nto geographical ideas, which are mostly relevant. (AO3)\n• Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical\nknowledge and understanding throughout. (AO1)\n• Critically investigates the question/issue to produce a\ncoherent analysis of data/evidence, making logical\nconnections to relevant geographical ideas. (AO3)\n11600 Pearson Edexcel Level 3 Advanced GCE in Geography – Sample Assessment Materials –\nIssue 2 – February 2017 © Pearson Education Limited 2017",
  "pageFrom": 5,
  "pageTo": 5,
