  "q": "2",
  "part": "a",
  "marks": 4,
  "topic": "geo-syn",
  "topicConfident": true,
  "inSpec": true,
  "text": "Table 1 below shows data for the level of development of the 38 members of the\nUnited Nations that are landlocked countries.\nA Chi-squared test was used to establish if there is an association between being\nlandlocked and the level of development. Two hypotheses were set up:\nThere is no significant association between the level of development and being\nlandlocked.\nAlternative hypothesis:\nThere is a significant association between the level of development and being\nlandlocked.\n(i) Using the formula and the data in Table 1 below calculate the value of χ 2\nχ2 ¦ \u000bO \u0010 E\f2\nE\nShow your working.\nχ 2 = ..............................................................\nLevel of Low human Medium human High human Very high\ndevelopment development development development human\nObserved 14 4 7 development\nExpected\n(O-E)2 9.50 9.50 9.50 13\n(O-E)2 / E\n20.25 30.25 6.25 9.50\n2.13 3.18 0.66 12.25\n1.29\nTable 1\n(ii) To test the significance of this value for χ 2 the table below was used to\ndetermine which hypothesis should be accepted.\n90% significant (0.1) 95% significant (0.05) 99% significant (0.01)\nCritical value 6.25 7.81 11.34\nState the result of this test of significance.",
  "ms": "Table 1 below shows data for the level of development of th 38 Mark\nnumber members of the United Nations that are landlocked countries. 4\nA Chi-squared test was used to establish whether the null or\nalternative hypothesis should be accepted. These hypotheses\nare:\nNull hypothesis:\nThere is no significant association between the variable of\nlevel of development and being landlocked\nAlternative hypothesis:\nThere is a significant association between the variable of\nlevel of development and being landlocked\n(i) Using the formula and the data in Table 1\nbelow calculate the value of X2\n(2)\nShow your working.\nX2 =\n………………………………………………………………..\nLevel of Low Human Medium High Human Very High\nDevelop Developmen Human Developmen Human\nment t Developmen t Developmen\nt t\nObserve 14 7\nd 4 13\nExpected 9.50 9.50\n(O-E)2 20.25 9.50 6.25 9.50\n(O-E)2 / E 2.13 30.25 0.66 12.25\n3.18 1.29\nTable 1\n(4)\nAO3 (2 marks)\n1 mark for correct method i.e. adding the four different values of (O-E)2 /E so\n2.13+3.18+0.66+1.29 (1)\n1 mark for correct result = 7.26 (1)\n(ii) To test the significance of this value for X2 the Mark\nnumber table below was used to determine which\nhypothesis should be accepted.\n90% 95% significant 99% significant\nsignificant (0.05) (0.01)\n(0.1)\nCritical 7.81 11.34\nValue 6.25\nState the result of this test of significance\n...............................................................................................................\n(2)\n(2)\nAO3 (2 marks)\nDo not double-penalise – accept the candidates answer to 2 (a) (i)\nOne mark for the significance level – the second mark for the\nacceptance/rejection of the hypotheses\nThe result is significant at the 90% level (1) so we can accept the alternative\nhypothesis (1)\nOr - The result is below the 95% level (1) therefore we reject the alternative\nhypothesis and/or accept the null hypothesis (1)\nAccept alternative versions of this result",
  "pageFrom": 3,
  "pageTo": 4,
